new, format is None) → default PNG. That matches old behavior (`or \"PNG\"`).\n\nHmm, \"rather than assuming they're always images\" — weird phrasing for PIL images since PIL images ARE always images. Maybe it means: use `Image.MIME.get(format)` mapping from PIL? PIL provides `PILImage.MIME` dict mapping format → MIME type (e.g., 'PNG' → 'image/png', 'JPEG' → 'image/jpeg', 'PDF' → 'application/pdf'!). Interesting — PIL can save PDFs! `PILImage.MIME['PDF']` = 'application/pdf'. So if a PIL image has format 'PDF' (a PDF opened via PIL... PIL can open PDFs? It can save PDFs; opening requires Ghostscript? Actually PIL can't read PDF). But PIL's MIME dict includes 'PDF': 'application/pdf'. That's likely what \"correctly identify and encode their format using proper MIME types rather than assuming they're always images\" refers to — use `PILImage.MIME` mapping so that e.g. format 'PDF' gets 'application/pdf' instead of 'image/pdf'.\n\nSo for PIL images: \n```python\nfile_format = (image.format or \"PNG\").upper()\nmime_type = PILImage.MIME.get(file_format, f\"image/{file_format.lower()}\")\n```\n\nThat's a nice approach. PIL's MIME dict: 'BMP': 'image/bmp', 'DIB': 'image/bmp', 'GIF': 'image/gif', 'JPEG': 'image/jpeg', 'PPM': 'image/x-portable-anymap'? Let me recall... Actually PIL Image.MIME:\n\n```python\nMIME = {\n    \"BMP\": \"image/bmp\",\n    \"DIB\": \"image/bmp\",\n    \"GIF\": \"image/gif\",\n    \"IFF\": \"image/iff\",\n    \"JPEG\": \"image/jpeg\",\n    \"JPG\": \"image/jpg\"?  # not sure\n    \"PNG\": \"image/png\",\n    \"TIFF\": \"image/tiff\",\n    \"PDF\": \"application/pdf\",\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nSomething like that. Using `PILImage.MIME.get(format, f\"image/{format.lower()}\")` is good.\n\nBut careful: when saving a PIL image with format=\"PDF\", the bytes are a PDF and mime application/pdf — consistent! Nice.\n\n### Data URI handling\n\nOld: `if image.startswith(\"data:image/\")`. New: any `data:` URI should be accepted. But should we validate it's a wel